
# Ripple — Real-Time Audience Engagement Platform

Ripple is a full-stack, microservices-based audience engagement
platform built with Java, Spring Boot, React, TypeScript, and PostgreSQL.

It allows organizers to host interactive events, launch live polls,
collect audience votes, and visualize results in real time.

## Features

### Organizer
- Account registration and login
- BCrypt password hashing
- Server-side session authentication
- Event creation and management
- Unique six-character event join codes
- Event lifecycle: DRAFT → LIVE → ENDED
- Create, start, and close polls
- Live voting results and analytics
- QR-code-based event sharing
- Session-based event ownership authorization

### Audience
- Join events using an event code or QR link
- Participate without an organizer account
- Vote on live polls
- View poll results
- Receive live poll updates

## Technology Stack

| Layer | Technologies |
|---|---|
| Backend | Java 21, Spring Boot, REST APIs |
| Frontend | React, TypeScript, Vite, Tailwind CSS |
| Database | PostgreSQL |
| Persistence | Spring Data JPA, Hibernate |
| Database Migration | Flyway |
| Real-Time Communication | WebSocket, STOMP |
| Authentication | BCrypt, HTTP Sessions |
| DevOps | Docker, Docker Compose, Maven |
| Testing | Maven, Spring Boot Tests |

## Architecture

Ripple consists of two backend microservices:

### Event Service (Port 8081)

Responsible for:
- Organizer registration and login
- Session authentication
- Event creation and lifecycle
- Event ownership authorization
- CSRF token management

### Interaction Service (Port 8082)

Responsible for:
- Poll creation and lifecycle
- Audience voting
- Vote aggregation
- Poll results
- WebSocket event broadcasting

Interaction Service communicates with Event Service
to verify event ownership before organizer operations.

Each microservice uses its own PostgreSQL database.

